WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:02.305
Lesson 3.5.

00:00:02.305 --> 00:00:05.060
Perpetual futures and margin on-chain.

00:00:05.060 --> 00:00:07.942
By the end, you'll understand perp positions well

00:00:07.942 --> 00:00:10.363
enough to use them for hedging and carry,

00:00:10.363 --> 00:00:12.842
and to see exactly why high leverage fails.

00:00:14.142 --> 00:00:15.432
Here's the plan.

00:00:15.432 --> 00:00:18.029
What a perpetual future actually is, and the

00:00:18.029 --> 00:00:20.637
two margin modes you can choose between.

00:00:20.637 --> 00:00:24.343
Mark price, index price, and funding, the three mechanics

00:00:24.343 --> 00:00:27.582
that keep a perp tracking spot with no expiry.

00:00:27.582 --> 00:00:29.671
The liquidation formula, and what it

00:00:29.671 --> 00:00:31.722
actually does at high leverage.

00:00:31.722 --> 00:00:34.957
And a full worked example, with real numbers, showing

00:00:34.957 --> 00:00:37.892
exactly how little room high leverage leaves you.

00:00:39.192 --> 00:00:40.557
What a perp actually is.

00:00:41.857 --> 00:00:44.232
Here's a perpetual future, in full.

00:00:44.232 --> 00:00:46.477
It tracks an asset's price, with no

00:00:46.477 --> 00:00:49.656
expiry date, unlike a traditional futures contract

00:00:49.656 --> 00:00:51.702
that settles on a fixed day.

00:00:51.702 --> 00:00:54.884
You post margin, collateral backing the position,

00:00:54.884 --> 00:00:57.557
and choose leverage, a multiplier on your

00:00:57.557 --> 00:00:59.957
exposure relative to that margin.

00:00:59.957 --> 00:01:03.888
Higher leverage means more exposure, and controls, per dollar

00:01:03.888 --> 00:01:07.882
posted, but leaves proportionally less room before liquidation.

00:01:09.182 --> 00:01:12.092
Here's the actual choice you make, before opening

00:01:12.092 --> 00:01:15.186
any perp position, between the two margin modes.

00:01:15.186 --> 00:01:18.536
Isolated margin risks only the margin you specifically

00:01:18.536 --> 00:01:20.972
put into that one position; a bad trade

00:01:20.972 --> 00:01:23.891
there can't touch the rest of your account.

00:01:23.891 --> 00:01:27.485
Cross margin instead backs every open position with your

00:01:27.485 --> 00:01:31.142
entire account balance, meaning one badly wrong trade can

00:01:31.142 --> 00:01:34.421
genuinely drain everything else you're holding open.

00:01:35.720 --> 00:01:36.810
Quick check.

00:01:36.810 --> 00:01:40.520
What's the actual difference between isolated and cross margin?

00:01:44.940 --> 00:01:47.634
The answer: isolated margin risks only

00:01:47.634 --> 00:01:50.420
that specific position's own margin.

00:01:50.420 --> 00:01:52.766
Cross margin puts your whole account

00:01:52.766 --> 00:01:54.985
behind every open position at once.

00:01:56.286 --> 00:01:57.941
Mark, index, and funding.

00:01:59.241 --> 00:02:01.213
Here's what these three actually do,

00:02:01.213 --> 00:02:03.486
and why each one matters differently.

00:02:03.486 --> 00:02:06.818
Index price is the reference, pulled from spot markets

00:02:06.818 --> 00:02:10.451
elsewhere, what the asset is genuinely worth right now.

00:02:10.451 --> 00:02:13.902
Mark price is a smoothed version used specifically for

00:02:13.902 --> 00:02:18.846
liquidation decisions, designed to resist brief, manipulated price spikes.

00:02:18.846 --> 00:02:21.959
And funding is a periodic payment, usually every

00:02:21.959 --> 00:02:24.817
hour or every eight hours, between longs and

00:02:24.817 --> 00:02:27.994
shorts, that keeps the perp's price from drifting

00:02:27.994 --> 00:02:29.836
too far from index over time.

00:02:31.136 --> 00:02:34.143
Worth being precise about why liquidation uses

00:02:34.143 --> 00:02:37.405
mark price specifically, not index price directly,

00:02:37.405 --> 00:02:40.136
since it's an easy detail to overlook.

00:02:40.136 --> 00:02:42.806
A brief, thin-liquidity spike on one venue could

00:02:42.806 --> 00:02:46.376
otherwise trigger liquidations that shouldn't happen at all.

00:02:46.376 --> 00:02:48.281
Mark price smooths that out.

00:02:48.281 --> 00:02:51.014
But it also means your liquidation can technically

00:02:51.014 --> 00:02:53.586
trigger at a slightly different number than the

00:02:53.586 --> 00:02:56.051
headline spot price you're watching elsewhere.

00:02:57.351 --> 00:02:58.441
Quick check.

00:02:58.441 --> 00:03:02.136
Which price actually triggers your liquidation, mark or index?

00:03:06.556 --> 00:03:09.639
The answer: mark price, specifically because it's

00:03:09.639 --> 00:03:12.723
smoothed to resist brief, manipulated spikes that

00:03:12.723 --> 00:03:15.066
index price alone wouldn't filter out.

00:03:16.366 --> 00:03:17.801
The liquidation formula.

00:03:19.101 --> 00:03:21.843
Here's the formula, and exactly what it does to

00:03:21.843 --> 00:03:24.485
your room to be wrong as leverage climbs.

00:03:24.485 --> 00:03:27.035
Take one, divided by your leverage.

00:03:27.035 --> 00:03:30.078
Subtract your maintenance margin, the minimum buffer

00:03:30.078 --> 00:03:32.330
the exchange requires you to keep.

00:03:32.330 --> 00:03:35.419
The result is roughly how far, in percentage terms,

00:03:35.419 --> 00:03:38.690
the price can move against you before liquidation.

00:03:38.690 --> 00:03:41.820
Higher leverage shrinks that first term directly, which

00:03:41.820 --> 00:03:44.335
shrinks your entire allowed margin for error.

00:03:45.634 --> 00:03:48.279
Here's the one sentence this formula actually

00:03:48.279 --> 00:03:50.637
proves, worth holding onto more than any

00:03:50.637 --> 00:03:52.719
specific number in this lesson.

00:03:52.719 --> 00:03:55.289
Leverage doesn't just magnify your gains,

00:03:55.289 --> 00:03:57.670
the part everyone already expects.

00:03:57.670 --> 00:04:00.047
It shrinks how wrong you're allowed to be,

00:04:00.047 --> 00:04:03.198
mechanically, by the exact same multiple, whether you're

00:04:03.198 --> 00:04:05.243
thinking about that trade-off or not.

00:04:06.543 --> 00:04:07.548
Worked example.

00:04:08.848 --> 00:04:12.346
Here's the five-times long, step by step, starting

00:04:12.346 --> 00:04:15.638
from an E.T.H. entry of three thousand dollars,

00:04:15.638 --> 00:04:19.093
at zero point five percent maintenance margin.

00:04:19.093 --> 00:04:22.048
One divided by five is zero point two.

00:04:22.048 --> 00:04:26.417
Subtract zero point five percent maintenance margin: zero point

00:04:26.417 --> 00:04:29.857
one nine five, or nineteen point five percent.

00:04:29.857 --> 00:04:33.185
At three thousand dollars entry, that's a liquidation price

00:04:33.185 --> 00:04:36.124
of roughly two thousand four hundred fifteen dollars.

00:04:37.425 --> 00:04:40.530
Now the twenty-times short, the exact same entry

00:04:40.530 --> 00:04:44.125
price, and watch how dramatically less room remains.

00:04:44.125 --> 00:04:48.905
The five-times long survives roughly a nineteen-point-five-percent drop.

00:04:48.905 --> 00:04:52.570
The twenty-times short is liquidated by a price rise

00:04:52.570 --> 00:04:55.750
of only about four point five percent, a move

00:04:55.750 --> 00:04:59.000
E.T.H. can genuinely make within a single hour.

00:05:00.300 --> 00:05:03.323
Worth being precise about what that four point

00:05:03.323 --> 00:05:06.926
five percent actually represents, in real market terms,

00:05:06.926 --> 00:05:09.220
not just as an abstract number.

00:05:09.220 --> 00:05:13.420
E.T.H., and most volatile crypto assets, can genuinely move

00:05:13.420 --> 00:05:17.620
that much within a single hour, during ordinary volatility,

00:05:17.620 --> 00:05:20.790
without any unusual news or event at all.

00:05:20.790 --> 00:05:24.443
Twenty-times leverage isn't a bet on direction alone; it's

00:05:24.443 --> 00:05:27.600
a bet that nothing routine happens in the meantime.

00:05:28.900 --> 00:05:29.990
Quick check.

00:05:29.990 --> 00:05:32.898
Roughly how far can a 10x long fall

00:05:32.898 --> 00:05:36.855
before liquidation, at a 0.5% maintenance margin?

00:05:41.275 --> 00:05:44.110
The answer: about nine point five percent.

00:05:44.110 --> 00:05:46.558
One divided by ten is ten percent,

00:05:46.558 --> 00:05:49.985
minus zero point five percent maintenance margin.

00:05:51.285 --> 00:05:52.195
The full curve.

00:05:53.495 --> 00:05:57.182
Here's that same formula, run across six different leverage

00:05:57.182 --> 00:06:00.316
multiples, all at the same zero point five percent

00:06:00.316 --> 00:06:03.512
maintenance margin, so you can see the actual shape

00:06:03.512 --> 00:06:06.270
of the curve, not just two points on it.

00:06:06.270 --> 00:06:08.569
At two times leverage, you can be wrong

00:06:08.569 --> 00:06:11.225
by roughly forty-nine and a half percent.

00:06:11.225 --> 00:06:14.145
At three times, thirty-two point eight percent.

00:06:14.145 --> 00:06:15.781
By ten times, you're down to

00:06:15.781 --> 00:06:17.435
nine and a half percent.

00:06:17.435 --> 00:06:19.412
And by fifty times, just one and

00:06:19.412 --> 00:06:21.328
a half percent, a move that can

00:06:21.328 --> 00:06:23.125
happen inside a single candle.

00:06:24.425 --> 00:06:27.547
Notice how the curve isn't a straight line down;

00:06:27.547 --> 00:06:30.924
it falls fastest exactly where most people first add

00:06:30.924 --> 00:06:33.900
leverage, between two times and ten times.

00:06:33.900 --> 00:06:36.070
Going from two times to five times

00:06:36.070 --> 00:06:39.035
costs you thirty percentage points of room.

00:06:39.035 --> 00:06:41.126
Going from twenty times to fifty

00:06:41.126 --> 00:06:43.390
times only costs another three.

00:06:43.390 --> 00:06:46.374
The most dangerous leverage decision is usually

00:06:46.374 --> 00:06:48.550
the early one, not the extreme one.

00:06:49.850 --> 00:06:51.075
Put another way.

00:06:51.075 --> 00:06:53.515
Doubling your leverage from two times to four

00:06:53.515 --> 00:06:56.095
times roughly halves your room to be wrong.

00:06:56.095 --> 00:06:58.182
Doubling it again, from four times to

00:06:58.182 --> 00:07:00.515
eight times, roughly halves it again.

00:07:00.515 --> 00:07:02.383
It's not a gentle slope; it's a

00:07:02.383 --> 00:07:04.485
halving, every time you double down.

00:07:05.785 --> 00:07:07.310
Before you open the position.

00:07:08.610 --> 00:07:10.909
Here's how you actually check your own

00:07:10.909 --> 00:07:14.092
liquidation price before opening anything, using this

00:07:14.092 --> 00:07:17.045
program's own calculator instead of guessing.

00:07:17.045 --> 00:07:20.301
You run defi_calc.py perp, passing your entry price,

00:07:20.301 --> 00:07:23.980
your chosen leverage, and your maintenance margin rate.

00:07:23.980 --> 00:07:26.332
It returns your exact liquidation price

00:07:26.332 --> 00:07:28.867
and the percentage move it represents.

00:07:28.867 --> 00:07:31.504
Compare that percentage against how far the

00:07:31.504 --> 00:07:33.902
asset has actually moved on an ordinary

00:07:33.902 --> 00:07:35.640
day, not just on a good one,

00:07:35.640 --> 00:07:38.337
before you decide the leverage is reasonable.

00:07:39.636 --> 00:07:42.119
Funding also matters more the longer you

00:07:42.119 --> 00:07:44.420
hold, so weigh it against your actual

00:07:44.420 --> 00:07:47.081
timeframe, not just the entry decision.

00:07:47.081 --> 00:07:49.322
A short hedge held for a few hours

00:07:49.322 --> 00:07:52.012
pays or receives only a few small funding

00:07:52.012 --> 00:07:54.681
settlements, a minor cost either way.

00:07:54.681 --> 00:07:57.679
The same position held open for months compounds

00:07:57.679 --> 00:08:00.677
every single settlement, and can quietly erode a

00:08:00.677 --> 00:08:02.941
position that looked fine on day one.

00:08:04.241 --> 00:08:05.976
Here's your full checklist.

00:08:05.976 --> 00:08:08.791
Do it now, before opening any perp position.

00:08:08.791 --> 00:08:13.331
Isolated margin, by default, every time, not cross margin.

00:08:13.331 --> 00:08:15.672
Liquidation price computed and known,

00:08:15.672 --> 00:08:17.636
before you open, not after.

00:08:17.636 --> 00:08:20.379
Leverage kept at two to three times, or less,

00:08:20.379 --> 00:08:23.601
for anything except a hedge you fully understand.

00:08:23.601 --> 00:08:26.283
And funding cost checked specifically for however

00:08:26.283 --> 00:08:27.946
long you actually plan to hold.

00:08:29.246 --> 00:08:30.491
Let's recap.

00:08:30.491 --> 00:08:32.752
A perp tracks price with no expiry;

00:08:32.752 --> 00:08:35.314
you post margin and choose leverage.

00:08:35.314 --> 00:08:38.096
Isolated margin risks only that position;

00:08:38.096 --> 00:08:40.914
cross margin risks your whole account.

00:08:40.914 --> 00:08:45.711
Mark price triggers liquidation specifically, smoothed against manipulation;

00:08:45.711 --> 00:08:48.939
funding keeps the perp tracking spot over time.

00:08:48.939 --> 00:08:52.085
And the liquidation formula proves the real lesson:

00:08:52.085 --> 00:08:54.687
leverage shrinks your room to be wrong, by

00:08:54.687 --> 00:08:57.772
the same multiple it magnifies your gains, whether

00:08:57.772 --> 00:08:59.829
you're accounting for that or not.

00:09:01.129 --> 00:09:04.440
Default to isolated margin, compute your liquidation

00:09:04.440 --> 00:09:07.188
price before opening, and keep leverage low

00:09:07.188 --> 00:09:09.862
unless you fully understand the hedge.

00:09:09.862 --> 00:09:17.057
Next up, Lesson 3.6: lending design deep dive.
